  "body": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Franschhoek is ridiculously beautiful. Put a very good restaurant in the middle of all that, give us a window table and a bottle of Chardonnay, and I’m already fairly easy to please. La Petite Colombe still managed to surprise me.\n\nI’d expected an impressive lunch. I hadn’t expected a transport relay, the entire restaurant to ourselves, or quite so much fun from a tasting menu. The setting at Leeu Estates is a considerable advantage, but the cooking earns its place at the centre of the afternoon."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A slightly improvised arrival"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Our booked taxi didn’t turn up. Secretly, we were quite pleased: we’d wanted an excuse to try one of Franschhoek’s tuk tuks. A bright blue one duly took us to Leeu Estates, where we discovered that tuk tuks aren’t allowed through the gates. So we transferred to a golf buggy for the final stretch to La Petite Colombe. Two vehicles for one lunch. Apparently we like to make an entrance.",
      "image1": "/uploads/arrival1.jpg",
      "image2": "/uploads/grounds2.jpg"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_pair",
      "image1": "/uploads/arrival1.jpg",
      "image2": "/uploads/grounds2.jpg"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A table for two. A restaurant for two."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Then came the rather wonderful discovery that we had the entire restaurant to ourselves. It’s only happened to us once before, at Huset in Svalbard, and it still feels faintly improbable. All that space, and just us settling in for lunch."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Our table was beside the large black-framed windows, looking across the grounds towards the Franschhoek mountains. Clouds rolled across them throughout the meal, continually changing the view. Between courses, there was no shortage of things to look at."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Inside, natural timber, muted greens and terracotta kept everything warm and comfortable. Plants and the views softened the polished edges. It was contemporary and design-led without making me feel I should sit up straighter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "And there was a real fireplace. It wasn’t lit, but it had clearly seen service on a recent cold evening: that lingering smell of wood smoke was still there. It’s one of my favourite smells. I’d love to come back on a cold Franschhoek evening and see that fire roaring."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Serious cooking, happily unserious presentation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The tasting menu was the main event, and I thought the food represented really good value. Given the standard of cooking, the amount of food and the work that went into its presentation, it felt generous. This was a substantial lunch, not a series of tiny gestures followed by a search for something to eat."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The opening bites arrived amongst greenery, herbs and edible flowers, with little picnic-like arrangements and enough detail to reward a second look. Then the practical business of eating them took over. Beautiful is lovely; beautiful and enjoyable is the point."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Theatre without the nonsense"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There was table-side preparation, there were little reveals, and there was mist drifting across the table. I’m very happy with a bit of theatre over lunch. My patience tends to depend on whether the food can hold its own once the smoke clears. Here, it could."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Bread came with a spread of accompaniments, while flowing white ceramics made even the tableware part of the fun. The meal moved from those intricate little bites into more substantial seafood and meat courses, with beautifully composed plates and plenty going on without losing its sense of purpose."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Playful rather than pretentious"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "I've eaten in restaurants where, somewhere around course eight, you realise you've stopped enjoying yourself. Another technically perfect plate arrives, somebody explains fourteen ingredients and something else has inevitably been fermented for six months. Eventually, you just want chips. La Petite Colombe never reached that point. The theatre complemented the cooking rather than compensating for it."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "One good bottle, please"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The wine pairing was where the pricing lost me. Against the very good value of the food menu, it seemed unnecessarily expensive. We decided against it and asked the sommelier to recommend a bottle instead."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "He suggested a lovely Chardonnay, and we were very happy with that decision. It worked well across the meal, and I enjoyed settling into one good bottle rather than continually moving between small pairing pours. Credit to him for listening to what we wanted and finding something that suited us, rather than nudging us towards the more expensive option."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Service without the ceremony"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That judgement ran through the service. With an entire restaurant team essentially looking after one table, there was plenty of scope for things to feel awkward. They never did. The team was warm, knowledgeable and relaxed, attentive without turning lunch into a performance we had to keep responding to."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Obviously, having the place to ourselves gave us an unusually personal afternoon. What impressed me was how comfortable they made it feel."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A little Wonka at the end"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The desserts kept the playfulness going: sculptural bowls, bright colours and tiny finishing touches, followed by petits fours that took the miniature-garden idea to its logical, chocolate-filled conclusion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Greenery, colourful sweets and tiny Tonka Bar elements put the final presentation somewhere between fine dining, a miniature garden and Willy Wonka. A very agreeable place to end up. By then, any suspicion that this might be an afternoon of solemn appreciation had thoroughly disappeared."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Beyond the table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Leeu Estates gives La Petite Colombe a rather extraordinary setting. The art isn’t confined to a lobby: large-scale sculptures appear amongst the vineyards and gardens, with mountains behind them and beautifully kept paths connecting it all."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "I loved that combination of art, planting and landscape. It gives the visit room to extend beyond the dining room without taking anything away from the restaurant. The meal is the reason to come; the grounds are a very good reason not to hurry away."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Impressive, yes. But also fun."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "La Petite Colombe could easily have been one of those technically impressive restaurants that I admired more than I enjoyed. Instead, it was genuinely fun: ambitious and theatrical without disappearing completely up its own backside."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The unexpected arrival set the mood, the window table and rolling clouds did their bit, and the cooking and service made it worth staying put. Having the restaurant to ourselves was a rare bonus. But the thing I’d come back for is that combination of serious food and a thoroughly good time. Preferably with the fire lit."
    }
  ],
  "scoreLabel": "9.5/10 · THE TRAVEL TAYLOR",
  "wouldGoBack": "Absolutely. Next time I'd happily skip booking the taxi altogether. Give me the tuk tuk, golf buggy, another bottle of Chardonnay and a cold Franschhoek evening with that fire roaring.",
